WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.960
An opioid emergency occurs when someone takes too much of a painkiller like oxycodone, fentanyl,

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:10.560
or heroin, which causes the part of their brain that controls breathing to slow down

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.720
or even stop completely.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:17.520
This is a critical situation that can quickly lead to cardiac arrest, so recognizing the

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.639
signs early is vital.

00:00:19.639 --> 00:00:24.240
You should suspect an overdose if you see drug paraphernalia nearby, or if the person

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.200
has a limp body, pinpoint pupils, and blue or gray-colored lips.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.080
Other major red flags include slow, shallow breathing, or the person becoming completely

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.639
unresponsive.

00:00:35.639 --> 00:00:41.200
If you do suspect an emergency, your first move is to call 911 and get an AED.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.959
If the person is not breathing at all, you must start CPR immediately.

00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:50.259
However, if they are still breathing, your priority is to administer naloxone, often

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:52.599
known by the brand name Narcan.

00:00:52.599 --> 00:00:58.119
To use Narcan, place the person on their back, insert the nozzle into one of their nostrils,

00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:00.160
and press the plunger with your thumb.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.680
Wait about two to three minutes for a response.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.199
If they still haven't woken up, you can give a second dose in the opposite nostril.

00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:11.879
It is important to know that Narcan has no serious side effects, so if you're ever in

00:01:11.879 --> 00:01:16.160
doubt, it is always safer to use it as it won't hurt someone who doesn't actually need

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:16.440
it.

